# vestibule — the entrance hall behind the great door, and the screen of two columns into the
# rotunda
#
# ================================================================================================
# WHAT THIS PART PUTS UNDER LOAD — do not "tidy" any of it away.
#
# THE WIDEST DYNAMIC RANGE IN THE BUILDING, ACROSS THREE METRES OF FLOOR.
#
# Outside the great door is a south-facing portico floor in full sun. Inside it is this room, and
# the ONLY DIRECT LIGHT IN IT is that door: 2.70 wide, 4.50 tall, at the bottom of a 0.90 m reveal,
# under five metres of portico roof that keeps the sun off the opening for most of the day. There
# is no window and no lamp. The one other opening is the screen at the back, and what comes through
# that is light that has already bounced off the rotunda — a different colour, a different
# direction and two or three stops down.
#
# So this room is where the two lighting regimes of the building meet, three metres apart: a hard
# sun patch on a polished floor at one end and a soft bounce off a lit hall at the other, with a
# nearly black ceiling between them. From the sunlit threshold to the back of a coffer is about
# four metres and something like twelve stops, and every one of them is in one frame. A path trace
# from the middle of this floor is the noisiest frame the building can produce; that is the point.
#
# That is the whole reason this room is shaped the way it is, and four things are stacked inside
# that gradient on purpose, in the order they get harder:
#
#   1. A POLISHED FLOOR THAT TAKES THE SUN PATCH. marble at rough=140 with a porphyry border and
#      eleven squares of contrasting stone, and the joints between them are PAINTED lines 0.03 m
#      wide — one voxel at the contract's metre 32, no geometry underneath to tell a denoiser
#      where the line goes. Half of that pattern is in direct sun and half is in shadow, in the
#      same square metre. This is the same trick walls.clip plays with its ashlar joints, put
#      where the exposure is hardest instead of where it is easiest.
#
#   2. A SCREEN OF TWO COLUMNS ON THE EDGE OF THE LIGHT. The two shafts stand on x = +-1.35, which
#      is exactly where the door's jambs are, so the edge of the beam coming through the door runs
#      DOWN THE MIDDLE OF EACH SHAFT: half of a fluted column in direct sun and the other half in
#      the room's own shadow, with the terminator crossing twelve flutes. A soft shadow edge on a
#      curved fluted surface is the hardest shading problem in the building, and it is the reason
#      the columns are on the jamb lines and not somewhere more comfortable.
#
#   3. DENTILS WITH A 0.03 m GAP, IN SHADOW. The screen's cornice carries 79 dentils 0.06 wide on
#      a 0.09 pitch. One voxel of gap, four and a half metres up, lit only by light that has
#      already bounced off the floor. If a denoiser is going to smear anything in this building it
#      will smear these first, and unlike the entablature outside there is no sun on them to help.
#
#   4. COFFERS OVER THE WHOLE OF IT. Five hollows 0.225 deep, moulded in three steps 0.0225 wide —
#      sub-voxel, exactly as the order intends them — with a boss left standing in each pan and
#      gilded. A gilt boss at the back of a hollow in the darkest part of the room is the hardest
#      thing in this fragment: a metal, so it needs a specular answer, at the bottom of the light
#      budget, behind an edge the sampler cannot resolve.
#
# Two niches in the side walls carry the fifth: the SOFT GRADIENT the contract names, a half-domed
# hollow whose only light is bounced twice.
#
# DO NOT ADD A LIGHT IN HERE. A sconce would make this room easy and it would delete the whole
# point of it. fittings.clip owns lamps; the vestibule is the one interior that must stay dark.
#
# AND IT IS NOT DARK, BECAUSE THERE IS A FIRST-FLOOR WINDOW IN THE CEILING. Measured, not guessed:
# the run of air at (x 3.0, z -6.0) is 10.062 m from this floor, closed at both ends, where the room
# is 4.95 — the ceiling slab is simply not there at x = ±2.20 .. ±3.30. It is there in
# `--part part_vestibule`, which applies no voids and measures 5.000 m at the same point, so it is
# somebody's cutter. It is windows.clip's piano nobile opening, 1.98 m deep against a 0.90 wall,
# and it is the fault `requests/walls-thickness.md` already exists about. It measures the SAME
# before and after everything below, so it is not the enlargement's doing. Written up with its
# second half — the sill at 6.30 is below this ceiling at 6.75 whatever the cutter depth is — in
# clips/facility/requests/vestibule-ceiling.md.
# ================================================================================================
#
# WALKABLE, AND THESE ARE THE MEASUREMENTS RATHER THAN THE INTENTION (2026-08-16).
#
# The player is 1.80 m standing, 0.60 m wide, and steps risers to 0.55. Every figure is `clipcheck`
# on the WHOLE BUILDING at metre 16, the `clear` line of `--gap` — the longest unbroken run of air
# with stone at both ends — with the control arm taken from `git archive` in the same session.
#
#   what                                   probe                          before   after
#   head on the axis, mid room             gap y@0,-6.0                    5.000    5.062
#   head 0.9 m in from the door            gap y@0,-6.9                    4.500    4.938
#   head at x 3.3                          gap y@3.3,-6.0                 10.062   10.062  *
#   great door, clear WIDTH                gap x@-0.70,-7.30               2.750    2.750
#   great door, clear HEAD                 gap y@0,-7.30                   4.500    4.500
#   screen, clear WIDTH between shafts     gap x@-0.70,-5.175              2.250    2.250
#   screen, clear HEAD under architrave    gap y@0,-5.175                  4.062    4.062
#   floor east-west at dado height         gap x@-1.30,-6.20               7.125    7.125
#   floor east-west above the dado         gap x@-0.70,-6.20               7.750    7.750
#   FLOOR NORTH-SOUTH, x = 0               gap z@0,-1.30                   3.250    3.250 **
#   FLOOR NORTH-SOUTH, x = 1.5             gap z@1.5,-1.30                 1.188    1.625
#   the same at plinth height              gap z@1.5,-1.60                 1.125    1.562
#   FLOOR NORTH-SOUTH, x = 2.5             gap z@2.5,-1.30                 1.625    2.062
#   FLOOR NORTH-SOUTH, x = 3.3             gap z@3.3,-1.30                 1.875    2.312
#
#   *  the hole in the ceiling, above. Identical in both arms.
#   ** open at the far end: at x = 0 the run goes on through the screen into the rotunda and the
#      probe box ends before the stone does. It is not a constriction, it is the doorway.
#
# THE FOUR NORTH-SOUTH FIGURES ARE WHY THIS ROOM WAS ENLARGED. 1.125 m at plinth height, in the
# band x 1.35 .. 1.6875 where the door architrave and the screen column stand opposite one another,
# is a corridor. A person is 0.60 wide, so it was passable; it was also the tightest thing in the
# building, in the room that is the way into it. It is 1.562 now, and the worst figure anywhere on
# this floor is 1.562 against a floor of 1.20.
#
# Nothing here is a lip: the floor is flat at 1.80 and the deepest thing cut into it is a 0.036 m
# worn saucer, a fifteenth of the 0.55 a player steps up without noticing.
#
# THE MODULE, and where every number came from.
#
#   0.45 = M, 0.90 = D. The room is 16 M wide in the clear, 11 M high in the clear, and the screen
#   order is EXACTLY HALF THE GREAT ORDER: a column of 4.05 (9 M) with a diameter of 0.45 (M), so
#   its own module is M/2 = 0.225 and everything on it is still a module fraction.
#
#   1.80    floor — podium_top, the contract's
#   2.70    dado top             1.80 + 2 M
#   5.85    screen column head   1.80 + 4.05, and the same line as the string course outside
#   6.30    great door head      doors.clip's, and the contract's; my architrave sits on it
#   6.75    ceiling soffit       5.85 + 0.90 (2 M) of entablature. See the note below.
#   6.955   back of a coffer     the coffer is cut from 6.73, 0.02 under the soffit, and is
#                                0.225 (M/2) deep, so 0.245 of the 0.45 slab is left over it
#   7.20    top of the ceiling slab
#
#   x -3.60 .. 3.60   the clear room, 16 M
#   x  3.60 .. 4.50   the side walls, 2 M thick, centred on the portico column axis at 4.05, their
#                     outer face landing exactly on the jamb of the side door at 4.50
#   z -7.05           the inner face of the south wall: the contract's 7.50 less ONE M, not two.
#                     IT IS NOT A MODULE MULTIPLE and it cannot be — 7.50 is not — so the room's
#                     depth is not either, and every number measured off it is marked.
#   z -5.40 .. -4.95  the screen: two columns of D = 0.45 on the axis z = -5.175
#
# WHY THE CEILING IS AT 6.75 AND NOT AT 6.30. The brief said "about 6.30". 6.30 leaves 4.50 of
# clear height, and 4.50 has to hold a column and its entablature: either the column loses its
# entablature, or the column comes down to 3.60 and its diameter becomes 0.40, which is not a
# module fraction of anything. 6.75 gives 4.95 = 11 M, which is 4.05 + 0.90 exactly — a column of
# 9 M and an entablature of 2 M — and it leaves 0.45 of wall over the door head for the door's own
# architrave, which 6.30 does not. One module of difference bought the whole interior order.
#
# WHY THE ROOM IS 2.10 DEEP IN THE CLEAR AND WAS 1.65, AND WHY THE 0.45 CAME OUT OF THE WALL.
#
# The old note said 1.20 and meant 1.65, and both were written as "because that is all there is":
# the contract puts the rotunda's inner radius at 4.80 on the origin and walls.clip's inner face at
# z = -6.60, which leaves 1.80 m between them on the centre line, and the screen takes 0.45 of it.
# That is true of the space BETWEEN two things somebody else fixed. It is not true of the room,
# because the wall on the south side of it is 0.90 thick and only half of that is doing anything.
#
# 1.65 m is not a room. Measured on the finished building at ankle height, the walkable strip in
# front of the screen came out **0.9075 m** between the door architrave's outer fascia at z = -6.42
# and the screen column's plinth at z = -5.5125, in the band x 1.35 .. 1.6875 where the two are
# opposite each other. A person is 0.60 wide, so it is passable and it is the width of a corridor
# in a service stair, in the room that is supposed to be the entrance to the building.
#
# So the south face of this room is now at z = -7.05, which is the contract's block_z less M. It is
# a RECESS into walls.clip's wall and not a change to it: that wall is 2 M thick and keeps 1 M of
# stone between this room and the portico, everywhere, and the recess is confined to x -3.60 .. 3.60
# and y 1.80 .. 6.75, which is the footprint of this room and nothing else. Checked rather than
# assumed, part by part, over exactly that box: part_walls fills 100% of it and NOTHING ELSE IS IN
# IT — not doors, not windows, not the stair, not the pilasters, not a fitting, not a neighbouring
# room. `doors_gd_void` already punches to z = -5.70, 1.35 m past the new face, so the great door's
# reveal shortens from 0.90 to 0.45 and no leaf, fascia or patera moves.
#
# It buys 0.45 m: the same measurement is now **1.3575 m**, the clear depth is 2.10, and the floor
# pattern did not have to move at all — the border simply went from 0.225 south and 0.75 north to
# 0.675 (1.5 M) on both, which is the plinth's own depth and the tidier number of the two.
#
# EVERY PIECE OF STONE IN THIS FILE STILL STANDS OUTSIDE r = 4.80 — the nearest is the north face
# of the screen at z = -4.95, with 0.15 to spare — so whatever the rotunda cuts for itself cannot
# eat any of it. If you widen this room, widen it in x, and stop at 4.50 because the side door is
# there; if you deepen it further, you are into the last 1 M of somebody else's wall and that IS a
# request rather than an edit.
#
# WHAT I LEFT FOR SOMEBODY ELSE. The north face of the screen entablature is a plain plane at
# z = -4.95. It is the rotunda's elevation, not mine, and mouldings run out from it would have
# been cut in half by the rotunda's own circle. Whoever builds the rotunda should treat that face
# as theirs.
#
# THE NUMBERS THAT ARE NOT DIMENSIONS. Everything the eye can measure above is a module multiple or
# a module fraction (0.015 = M/30 is the finest, and it is the entablature's own sixtieth). These
# are tolerances and are never seen:
#
#   1.70, 1.75, -7.10   0.05 and 0.10 buried in the floor or the south wall — the brief's overlap
#   -7.55, -7.00        the sill slab, 0.05 past the wall's outer face and 0.05 under the floor
#   -4.725              where the floor runs out under the screen's plinths, which overhang it
#   6.73, 6.80          0.02 under the ceiling soffit, 0.05 over it: a cut past an arris, and a join
#   2.72, 3.55, 3.555   where the wall's true half laps its wandering half, and the two limits the
#                       wander is clipped between — see the displacement over the side walls
#   0.036, 0.396        the depth of the worn hollows and the semi-axis that makes it, which is a
#                       construction: 2.16 - 0.396 = 1.764, and 1.80 - 1.764 is the number meant
#   0.006               the capital's knit, a fifth of a voxel — see the screen, below
#   0.02 / 0.035        every paint rule's `below=`; 0.035 wherever a transform or a displacement
#                       put the shape where it is, which is BRIEF.md rule 5

include "_contract.clip"

# ================================================================================================
# THE FLOOR — a slab under the room, its top on the podium's own 1.80
# ================================================================================================
#
# The podium is already solid to 1.80 here, so this adds no visible matter: it exists so that the
# part stands on something of its own, and so the pattern below has a shape of mine to key on. It
# runs out to x = +-4.50 under the side walls and back to z = -4.725 under the screen's plinths,
# which overhang the beam.
let vestibule_floor = box -4.50 1.35 -7.10   4.50 1.80 -4.725

# The threshold, through the 0.45 reveal of the great door and out to the wall face. doors.clip's
# void opens the wall from -7.95, so this is the stone a foot lands on coming in. It laps the floor
# slab above by 0.05, which is the brief's overlap and is buried.
let vestibule_sill = box -1.35 1.35 -7.55   1.35 1.80 -7.00

# ================================================================================================
# THE WEAR — the two hollows a quarter of a million feet have worn into this floor
# ================================================================================================
#
# A threshold that has been walked over for two hundred years has a saucer worn into it, and so has
# the strip of floor between two doors that everybody crosses. It is the single cheapest true thing
# a scanned interior has that a modelled one does not, and this is the room to put it in: the great
# door is the only way into the building and the screen is the only way out of this room, so every
# foot that has ever been in here has crossed both of these two patches and nothing else.
#
# 0.036 at the deepest, struck as very oblate ellipsoids so the fall is smooth. A player steps up
# risers of 0.55 without noticing; this is a fifteenth of one and it slopes, so it cannot be a lip
# and cannot be tripped over. The threshold's is 1.80 across (the door's own 2.70 less a foot of
# jamb either side) and the screen's is 2.25, between the two column plinths.
#
# THEY ARE IN `void_vestibule` AND NOT SUBTRACTED FROM MY OWN FLOOR, and that is not a stylistic
# choice. This room's floor slab is 0.45 of stone lying on a podium that is SOLID to 1.80 under all
# of it — measured, `part_podium` fills 100.00% of the band y 1.72 .. 1.80 under this room — so a
# hollow taken out of my slab is backfilled by the podium in the same voxel and the floor comes
# back flat. A hollow in the floor of a room is room air, and the void is what room air goes in.
#
# BOUNDED SO THEY CANNOT REACH doors.clip. `doors_plinth_band` runs y 1.71 .. 2.16 at z -7.68 ..
# -7.35 and its lower 0.09 is deliberately buried under the opening as the stone every leaf laps
# into. Both hollows stop at z = -7.30, which is 0.05 clear of it.
# The two OVERLAP, and they have to: a worn floor is one continuous lane from the door to the
# screen, not two saucers with a strip of pristine stone between them. The sill's reaches to -6.76
# and the lane's starts at -6.95, so they run into one another and the fall is smooth all the way.
let vestibule_wear_sill = ellipsoid 0 2.16 -7.03  rx=0.90  ry=0.396 rz=0.27
let vestibule_wear_walk = ellipsoid 0 2.16 -6.15  rx=1.125 ry=0.396 rz=0.80
let vestibule_wear = intersection {
    union { vestibule_wear_sill vestibule_wear_walk }
    box -1.20 1.755 -7.30   1.20 1.81 -5.40
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE SIDE WALLS — 2 M thick, with a niche in each
# ================================================================================================
#
# From 1.75 (0.05 into the floor) to 7.20, the top of the ceiling slab. Their north end at -4.95
# is the anta of the screen; their south end runs 0.05 into the wall walls.clip built.
let vestibule_wall_e = box 3.60 1.75 -7.10   4.50 7.20 -4.95  round=0.015

# THE NICHE. 0.60 across (4M/3), 0.30 deep (2M/3), from the dado top at 2.70 to 4.50, half round in
# plan and half domed at the head — so it is a hollow with no flat surface in it anywhere and no
# direct light on any of it. This is the contract's "soft gradient inside a niche", put in the one
# room where there is no direct light to help it.
#
# Centred at z = -6.225, which is the middle of the 1.65 m of wall the room actually shows: the wall
# runs to -4.95 but its last 0.45 is the anta. It is NOT on the coffer's centre line at -6.375, and
# it should not be — the coffer is centred in the ceiling the cornice leaves and the niche in the
# wall the anta leaves, and those are two different pieces of wall. It moved 0.225 south with the
# room, and the 0.225 between the two centre lines is what stops them reading as one axis.
let vestibule_niche_shaft = cylinder 3.60 3.45 -6.225  r=0.30 h=1.50 axis=y
let vestibule_niche_head  = sphere   3.60 4.20 -6.225  r=0.30
let vestibule_niche_e     = union { vestibule_niche_shaft vestibule_niche_head }

# THE ARCHITRAVE ROUND IT. A niche 0.60 across in a plaster wall with no dressing at all is a
# gouge; what makes it read as a niche is the band round its mouth, and the band has to have a
# SECTION rather than being a flat plate. So it is the shell between a cutter 0.135 (0.3 M) larger
# and the cutter itself, taken where it crosses the wall face — which follows the jamb up and round
# the half dome on its own, with no arithmetic per member, and returns across the sill as its base.
# The same construction rotunda.clip uses round its four niches, at this room's smaller scale.
#
# 0.135 wide and standing 0.09 (M/5) proud. The projection is the number that matters twice over.
# Once because a member that projects one voxel is a member that is not there — this file's own
# entablature note measures that failure twice. And once because the wall behind it now WANDERS by
# up to 0.045 (see the displacement below, which is clipped so it can come no further into the room
# than that): a band at the 0.045 an undisturbed wall would want could be swallowed by its own
# plaster, and at 0.09 it stands at least 0.045 proud of the worst of it, everywhere.
let vestibule_niche_big  = union {
    cylinder 3.60 3.45 -6.225  r=0.435 h=1.77 axis=y
    sphere   3.60 4.20 -6.225  r=0.435
}
let vestibule_niche_band = box 3.51 2.565 -6.75   3.69 4.725 -5.70
let vestibule_niche_dr_e = intersection {
    difference { vestibule_niche_big vestibule_niche_e }
    vestibule_niche_band
}

let vestibule_wall_cut_e = difference { vestibule_wall_e vestibule_niche_e }

# THE PLASTER IS NOT A MATHEMATICAL PLANE, AND THIS IS THE ONE LINE THAT SAYS SO.
#
# These two walls are 1.65 x 4.95 m of flat render each, they are the largest unbroken surface in
# the room, and the whole point of this fragment is that they are lit by a beam of sun a metre and
# a half away and by nothing else. A dead-flat plane under a grazing light is the one thing that
# reads instantly as computer graphics: real float-finished plaster on real brick undulates by two
# or three centimetres over a metre and the light crawls along it.
#
# 0.030 is 0.96 of a voxel at the contract's metre 32 — just past the sampler's half-voxel floor,
# so the surface genuinely moves rather than dithering, which is the failure the manifest's own
# 0.012 grain is dropped for. `grain_broad` (fbm size=0.90) and not a fine noise: a long wavelength
# is a float mark, a short one is sandpaper.
#
# DISPLACED BEFORE THE MIRROR, SO THE TWO WALLS STAY IDENTICAL. A `mirror` folds x to |x| and hands
# the same child to both sides, so a displacement inside it is sampled at the same coordinate twice
# and the room stays symmetric; put it outside and the noise is evaluated at +x and -x separately
# and the two sides drift apart, which is exactly what BRIEF.md rule 3 forbids the shell.
#
# AND IT IS CLIPPED BACK TO A BOX ON FIVE OF ITS SIX FACES. A displacement moves EVERY surface of
# the shape it is given, and five of this wall's six are datums somebody else lands on: x = 4.50 is
# the jamb of the side door, z = -4.95 is the anta line the screen entablature dies into, z = -7.10
# and y = 1.75 are the 0.05 laps into walls.clip and into the floor, and y = 7.20 is the top of the
# ceiling slab. Only the face that looks into the room is allowed to wander, so the intersection
# squares the other five off again wherever the noise pushed them out. Where it pushes them IN it
# cannot open anything: the two laps are 0.05 and the worst case leaves 0.02 of them.
#
# AND IT STARTS AT THE DADO TOP AND NOT AT THE FLOOR. Everything below 2.70 has a member standing
# on it — the dado body 0.045 proud, its cap 0.09, the skirting 0.135 — and a wall face allowed to
# wander 0.045 into the room would have swallowed the first of those and half of the second. So the
# bottom 0.90 is left true, the wander is confined to the 4.05 m of open plaster above it, and the
# step between the two lands at 2.70 where the dado's own cap stands 0.09 in front of it and hides
# it. The two halves lap by 0.02 so they are certainly one solid.
let vestibule_wall_true = intersection {
    vestibule_wall_cut_e
    box 3.55 1.70 -7.15   4.50 2.72 -4.95
}
let vestibule_wall_wob = intersection {
    displace { vestibule_wall_cut_e grain_broad } amount=0.030
    box 3.555 2.70 -7.15   4.50 7.20 -4.95
}
let vestibule_wall_render = union { vestibule_wall_true vestibule_wall_wob }
let vestibule_walls       = mirror { vestibule_wall_render } axis=x
let vestibule_niches      = mirror { vestibule_niche_e } axis=x
let vestibule_niche_dress = mirror { vestibule_niche_dr_e } axis=x

# ================================================================================================
# THE SCREEN — two columns of the order at half size, on the axis z = -5.175
# ================================================================================================
#
# `ionic_column` is 9 D tall and there is nowhere in this building's interior to stand one, so the
# screen takes the same column at exactly half: 4.05 tall, 0.45 at the foot, its own module M/2.
# Halving it and not redrawing it is deliberate — a redrawn column drifts away from the great order
# the first time somebody changes a moulding, and this one cannot.
#
# It costs the flutes their comfortable size: the flat fillet between two of them comes down from
# 0.030 to 0.015, and the volute's tube from 0.045 to 0.0225. That is HALF A VOXEL and A THIRD OF
# ONE at the contract's metre 32, which is past the point the order's own note defends. It is
# still the right answer here — the alternative is a screen of two smooth posts — and it is a
# genuine question put to the sampler rather than a rounding error: this is the one place in the
# building where the geometry is finer than the grid on purpose.
#
# `scale` is given no bounding box by the field, on purpose, because uneven scaling can report less
# than its own box distance. That means a scaled column cannot be culled and every voxel of my
# bounds would walk the whole column expression. Wrapping it in an intersection with a box of its
# own puts the box back, soundly, because a box is square: 4.7 million field evaluations became
# 0.9 million.
let vestibule_col_body = intersection {
    scale { ionic_column } x=0.5 y=0.5 z=0.5
    box -0.40 -0.02 -0.40   0.40 4.07 0.40
}

# The shoe and the bed: the same argument portico.clip makes. The column's plinth is 0.675 square
# and its abacus 0.54 by 0.36, so a block 0.60 square straddling the floor line and one 0.30 by
# 0.24 straddling the architrave line are inside stone that is already there at both ends, and the
# joins are certain rather than coincident planes.
let vestibule_col_shoe = box -0.30 -0.05 -0.30   0.30 0.05 0.30  round=0.012
let vestibule_col_bed  = box -0.15  4.03 -0.12   0.15 4.10 0.12

# AND ONE MORE THING THE HALF SIZE COSTS. The volute's tube is 0.0225 across down here — under a
# voxel — and at some phases of the grid it sheds a voxel off the outside of the scroll and the
# part reports three components instead of one. The capital alone, and nothing else, is grown by
# 0.006 — a fifth of a voxel, less than the manifest's own grain — which knits the scroll back
# together without moving a surface the eye can measure. Do not grow the shaft with it: 0.006 on
# the shaft would be 0.006 off every flute.
let vestibule_col_knit = intersection {
    round { vestibule_col_body } by=0.006
    box -0.40 3.78 -0.40   0.40 4.07 0.40
}

let vestibule_col_unit = union {
    vestibule_col_body vestibule_col_knit vestibule_col_shoe vestibule_col_bed
}
# ON x = +-1.35 AND NOWHERE ELSE. Three things land on that pair of numbers at once: the jambs of
# the great door, the inner pair of portico columns outside, and the 2.70 (3 D) eustyle bay the
# contract calls the spine of the elevation. So the screen's intercolumniation is the building's
# own bay, the columns stand on the same two axes as the columns you walked between to get here,
# and the door is framed by them exactly. It also puts each shaft half in the door's light and
# half out of it, which is the load note's second item.
let vestibule_col_east = translate { vestibule_col_unit } 1.35 1.80 -5.175
let vestibule_columns  = mirror { vestibule_col_east } axis=x

# THE ANTAE. The screen is in antis: the side walls end in a pier 0.45 (D of the small order) wide
# and the entablature lands on it. A pilaster of the order would be 0.6875 across at this scale and
# there is only 0.45 of wall to put it on, so the anta is the wall's own end, given the projection
# and the cap the columns have and nothing else.
let vestibule_anta_shaft = box 3.51 1.75 -5.40   3.60 5.805 -4.95 round=0.010
let vestibule_anta_plinth= box 3.465 1.75 -5.40  3.60 1.935 -4.95 round=0.010
let vestibule_anta_ovolo = ovolo 3.51 5.805 -5.40   3.465 5.715 -4.95 run=z
let vestibule_anta_fil   = box 3.465 5.805 -5.40  3.60 5.85 -4.95 round=0.008
let vestibule_anta_e     = union {
    vestibule_anta_shaft vestibule_anta_plinth vestibule_anta_ovolo vestibule_anta_fil
}
let vestibule_antae = mirror { vestibule_anta_e } axis=x

# ================================================================================================
# THE ENTABLATURE OVER THE SCREEN — 0.90 m, which is 2 M and a quarter of the column
# ================================================================================================
#
# Divided in sixtieths of itself (0.015 = M/30), the way the great entablature is divided in
# twentieths of its own:
#
#   5.850 - 6.150   ARCHITRAVE   two fasciae of 0.12, the lower set back 0.045 behind the upper,
#                                and a cyma reversa of 0.06 crowning them onto the frieze
#   6.150 - 6.450   FRIEZE       plain, flush at z = -5.40
#   6.450 - 6.750   CORNICE      bed mould, dentils, cap, ovolo, corona
#
# THE SMALLEST STEP IN IT IS 0.045, AND THAT IS A FLOOR, NOT A CHOICE. The first pass stepped the
# architrave in three fasciae 0.03 apart and gave the cornice's ovolo 0.015 of projection, which is
# what the proportions of the great order say at this size. Measured on its own at metre 32,
# `vestibule_ent_ovolo` came back **0 components, 0 voxels**, and so did the architrave's crown: a
# moulding is only the swell in FRONT of its core, so a member that projects one voxel is half a
# voxel of solid and it misses every voxel centre it passes. A one-voxel FEATURE is the point of
# this building; a one-voxel PROJECTION is a member of the order that is not there. The dentil gap
# is still 0.030, because a gap is a hole and a hole one voxel wide is a hole.
#
# So the fasciae went from three to two to buy each step 0.045, and the corona was let out to 0.30
# from the frieze — which then made the ceiling behind it 0.90 exactly, and the coffers fit.
#
# The core runs the full width x -4.50 .. 4.50 so that it is buried in the side walls at both ends
# and the whole part is certainly one piece; the mouldings stop at the wall face, x = +-3.60.
let vestibule_ent_f1    = box -4.50 5.85 -5.31   4.50 5.97 -4.95
let vestibule_ent_f2    = box -4.50 5.97 -5.355  4.50 6.09 -4.95
let vestibule_ent_fback = box -4.50 6.08 -5.355  4.50 6.15 -4.95
let vestibule_ent_crown = cyma_reversa -3.60 6.09 -5.355   3.60 6.15 -5.40 run=x

# The frieze, and everything above it that does not project: one block, 6.15 to 6.80. It runs
# 0.05 past the ceiling soffit at 6.75 so the slab above certainly meets it.
let vestibule_ent_core = box -4.50 6.15 -5.40   4.50 6.80 -4.95 round=0.012

# The bed mould needs no backing of its own: a moulding stands on a core at its FIRST corner's
# face, and the frieze block above is already at -5.40. A backing at -5.445 would have buried it
# whole, which is what the first pass did.
let vestibule_ent_bed   = cyma_reversa -3.60 6.45 -5.40   3.60 6.495 -5.475 run=x
let vestibule_ent_dband = box -4.50 6.495 -5.475  4.50 6.585 -4.95

# THE DENTILS. 0.06 wide (M/7.5) on a pitch of 0.09 (M/5), so the gap is 0.030 — M/15, the same
# number as the flat fillet between two flutes on a column, and ONE VOXEL at metre 32. There are
# 79 of them and every one of them is in shadow. Read the load note at the top of this file before
# widening that gap; it is the reason this cornice exists.
let vestibule_ent_dentil  = box -0.03 6.495 -5.55   0.03 6.585 -5.40 round=0.006
let vestibule_ent_dentils = repeat { vestibule_ent_dentil } x=0.09 nx=39

let vestibule_ent_dcap  = box -4.50 6.585 -5.595   4.50 6.615 -4.95 round=0.008
# The ovolo's own core, standing at its first corner's face and no further out.
let vestibule_ent_oback = box -4.50 6.615 -5.595   4.50 6.66  -4.95
let vestibule_ent_ovolo = ovolo -3.60 6.66 -5.595   3.60 6.615 -5.64 run=x
let vestibule_ent_corona= box -4.50 6.66 -5.70     4.50 6.75 -4.95 round=0.008

let vestibule_entab = union {
    vestibule_ent_f1 vestibule_ent_f2 vestibule_ent_fback vestibule_ent_crown
    vestibule_ent_core
    vestibule_ent_bed vestibule_ent_dband vestibule_ent_dentils
    vestibule_ent_dcap vestibule_ent_oback vestibule_ent_ovolo vestibule_ent_corona
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE CEILING — soffit on 6.75, five coffers in it
# ================================================================================================
#
# A slab 0.45 (M) thick over the whole room and both walls, running 0.05 into the south wall.
let vestibule_ceiling_slab = box -4.50 6.75 -7.10   4.50 7.20 -4.95 round=0.015

# ONE COFFER, drawn as the hollow it is, from y = 0 upward. 0.90 (D) across and 0.45 (M) deep in z,
# 0.225 (M/2) into the slab, stepping back in three courses 0.045 tall whose faces are 0.0225 wide.
# 0.0225 is under a voxel at metre 32, and unlike the entablature's mouldings that is fine and
# deliberate: this is a STEP IN A HOLLOW, not a projection off a face, so what it costs when the
# grid misses it is a softer arris rather than a member that vanishes. It is the same sub-voxel
# edge the order puts in `coffer_unit`, kept at the order's size rather than scaled with the room.
#
# AND IT IS NOW SQUARE, WHICH IS WHAT THE DEEPER ROOM BOUGHT.
#
# It used to be 0.90 by 0.45 — an oblong coffer, which no order has — and the note here used to
# explain why: the ceiling it hangs in was 0.90 clear between the south wall at -6.60 and the corona
# at -5.70, and 0.45 of that had to be band. With the south face at -7.05 the clear soffit is 1.35,
# so the coffer is the order's own 0.90 SQUARE (`coffer_unit`'s proportion) with 0.45 (M) of band
# east and west and 0.225 (M/2) north and south. The east-west band is the classical 2:1 exactly;
# the north-south is half of it, and 1.35 is what there is. An oblong coffer is a mistake anybody
# can see and a narrow band is one only a draughtsman can, which is the trade taken here.
let vestibule_cof_a    = box -0.45   0.000 -0.45     0.45   0.045 0.45    round=0.012
let vestibule_cof_b    = box -0.4275 0.045 -0.4275   0.4275 0.090 0.4275  round=0.012
let vestibule_cof_c    = box -0.405  0.090 -0.405    0.405  0.135 0.405   round=0.012
let vestibule_cof_pan  = box -0.36   0.130 -0.36     0.36   0.225 0.36    round=0.012
let vestibule_cof_rose = box -0.09   0.150 -0.09     0.09   0.225 0.09    round=0.018

let vestibule_coffer = difference {
    union { vestibule_cof_a vestibule_cof_b vestibule_cof_c vestibule_cof_pan }
    vestibule_cof_rose
}

# Five of them on a pitch of 1.35 (3 M), so the band between two is 0.45 (M) — the classical 2:1 —
# and the outermost is 0.45 clear of the wall. The seed sits at x = 0 so that no copy straddles a
# cell boundary, which would make the repeat infinitely slack and stop the sampler skipping.
# Dropped 0.02 below the soffit so the opening is cut past the rounding of its own first arris.
let vestibule_coffers = repeat { translate { vestibule_coffer } 0 6.73 -6.375 } x=1.35 nx=2

# The bosses, named again so they can be gilded. They add no matter — they are what the coffer
# leaves standing — and they hang 0.075 (M/6) below the back of the pan.
let vestibule_ros_seed = box -0.09 6.880 -6.465   0.09 6.955 -6.285 round=0.018
let vestibule_rosettes = repeat { vestibule_ros_seed } x=1.35 nx=2

let vestibule_ceiling = difference { vestibule_ceiling_slab vestibule_coffers }

# ================================================================================================
# THE DADO — 0.90 (2 M) high, round the whole room
# ================================================================================================
#
# Projecting 0.045 (M/10) from the wall, with an ovolo and a fillet capping it at 2.70. It starts
# at 1.70, below the floor, so it is certainly joined to it.
#
# The body runs the FULL height of the dado including the ovolo's band, not up to the ovolo's foot.
# A moulding is only the swell in front of its own first corner's face; drawn without a core behind
# it, the ovolo was a ribbon floating 0.045 off the wall, which is what the component count found.
let vestibule_dado_s      = box -3.60 1.70 -7.05   3.60 2.655 -7.005 round=0.010
let vestibule_dado_s_ov   = ovolo -3.60 2.655 -7.005   3.60 2.565 -6.96 run=x
let vestibule_dado_s_cap  = box -3.60 2.655 -7.05   3.60 2.70 -6.96 round=0.008

let vestibule_dado_e      = box 3.555 1.70 -7.05   3.60 2.655 -5.40 round=0.010
let vestibule_dado_e_ov   = ovolo 3.555 2.655 -7.05   3.51 2.565 -5.40 run=z
let vestibule_dado_e_cap  = box 3.51 2.655 -7.05   3.60 2.70 -5.40 round=0.008
let vestibule_dado_east   = union { vestibule_dado_e vestibule_dado_e_ov vestibule_dado_e_cap }

# ================================================================================================
# AND THE SKIRTING UNDER IT — a plinth with a section, which the dado never had
# ================================================================================================
#
# A dado that runs straight into the floor is a board, not a dado. Every one ever built stands on a
# plinth, and the plinth is where the moulding is: the wall is telling you it has a thickness and a
# footing, at exactly the height a person looks when they walk into a room and check the floor.
#
#   1.800 - 1.935   plinth    0.3 M    standing 0.135 (0.3 M) proud of the wall — three times the
#                             dado's own 0.045, which is what makes it read as a plinth and not as
#                             another fascia of the dado
#   1.935 - 2.025   congé     M/5      a cavetto dying back onto the dado's face at 3.555 / -7.005
#
# It starts at 1.70, under the floor, for the same reason the dado does: 0.10 buried is a certain
# join and a coincident plane is not.
#
# THE CAVETTO'S CORNERS ARE (face, bottom) THEN (projection, top), which is the order this whole
# building writes a moulding in: the first corner is in the stone and the second is in the air. The
# south run and the two side runs are the same section three times, turned — the `run=` key is what
# says which way the section is read, and it is not cosmetic: with the wrong one the section is
# drawn in the wrong plane and the moulding runs the wrong way along the wall.
let vestibule_skirt_s     = box -3.60 1.70 -7.05   3.60 1.935 -6.915 round=0.010
let vestibule_skirt_s_cav = cavetto -3.60 1.935 -7.005   3.60 2.025 -6.915 run=x
let vestibule_skirt_e     = box 3.465 1.70 -7.05   3.60 1.935 -5.40 round=0.010
let vestibule_skirt_e_cav = cavetto 3.555 1.935 -7.05   3.465 2.025 -5.40 run=z
let vestibule_skirt_east  = union { vestibule_skirt_e vestibule_skirt_e_cav }

let vestibule_dado = union {
    vestibule_dado_s vestibule_dado_s_ov vestibule_dado_s_cap
    vestibule_skirt_s vestibule_skirt_s_cav
    mirror { vestibule_dado_east } axis=x
    mirror { vestibule_skirt_east } axis=x
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE DOOR ARCHITRAVE — the great door seen from inside
# ================================================================================================
#
# 0.45 (M) wide, three fasciae each stepping 0.045 further out of the wall than the one nearer the
# opening — the same rule and the same three steps doors.clip uses on the outside of this door —
# and an astragal on the inner arris. It runs from the floor to 6.30, which is where doors.clip's
# void stops, and its head fills 6.30 to 6.75, which is where my ceiling starts. That exact fit is
# the reason the ceiling is at 6.75: at 6.30 there would have been no head at all.
let vestibule_arch_j1 = box 1.35  1.70 -7.05   1.53 6.30 -6.96  round=0.010
let vestibule_arch_j2 = box 1.53  1.70 -7.05   1.665 6.30 -6.915 round=0.010
let vestibule_arch_j3 = box 1.665 1.70 -7.05   1.80 6.30 -6.87  round=0.010
let vestibule_arch_jamb = union { vestibule_arch_j1 vestibule_arch_j2 vestibule_arch_j3 }

let vestibule_arch_h1 = box -1.53 6.30  -7.05   1.53 6.48  -6.96  round=0.010
let vestibule_arch_h2 = box -1.665 6.48 -7.05   1.665 6.615 -6.915 round=0.010
let vestibule_arch_h3 = box -1.80 6.615 -7.05   1.80 6.75  -6.87  round=0.010

# The astragal on the inner arris, drawn as a plate and rounded into a bead — doors.clip's trick,
# and the reason it is here is that a 0.045 bead is one and a half voxels and it runs the whole
# 4.50 m height of the opening, straight down the edge where the light changes fastest.
let vestibule_arch_plate = union {
    box 1.35 1.70  -6.9825   1.3725 6.3225 -6.9825
    box 0    6.30  -6.9825   1.3725 6.3225 -6.9825
}
let vestibule_arch_bead = round { mirror { vestibule_arch_plate } axis=x } by=0.0225

let vestibule_architrave = union {
    mirror { vestibule_arch_jamb } axis=x
    vestibule_arch_h1 vestibule_arch_h2 vestibule_arch_h3
    vestibule_arch_bead
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE PART
# ================================================================================================

let vestibule_mass = union {
    vestibule_floor vestibule_sill
    vestibule_walls vestibule_niche_dress
    vestibule_columns vestibule_antae vestibule_entab
    vestibule_ceiling
    vestibule_dado vestibule_architrave
}

let part_vestibule = vestibule_mass

# What this part removes from the building: the air of the room itself, and the two hollows worn
# into its floor. The air is the gross volume of the room less everything I built in it, so it takes
# away anything ANOTHER fragment leaves standing in here — the rotunda's wall, most likely, which
# has every reason to want the ground the screen stands on — while it cannot touch a stone of mine.
# The coffers and the niches are not in it: they are cut out of my own slab and my own walls, above,
# so the manifest never has to know they exist.
#
# THE AIR NOW REACHES z = -7.05 AND IT USED TO STOP AT -6.60, which is the 0.45 of walls.clip's
# 2 M wall this room is recessed into. That is the one line in this file that removes somebody
# else's stone, it is the whole of the enlargement, and the header says what was measured to be
# sure it is the only thing in there. It cannot reach a fitting: the manifest unions `part_fittings`
# back on AFTER every room void, which is what D608 is written up for.
let vestibule_air = box -3.60 1.80 -7.05   3.60 6.75 -4.95
let void_vestibule = union { difference { vestibule_air vestibule_mass }  vestibule_wear }

# ================================================================================================
# PAINT
# ================================================================================================
#
# Order matters: a later coat covers an earlier one. Shell first, then the floor built up from its
# field to its inlay, then the stone that stands on the floor, then gilt last of all because the
# bosses are inside the ceiling. `below=0.02` on every rule and never 0 — the manifest displaces
# the whole building by 12 mm at the end, and a test at zero misses the voxels that displacement
# made and leaves a pale rind of the base coat over the part.

# --- the shell -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `below=0.035` ON THE WALLS AND NOT 0.02, BECAUSE THEY ARE DISPLACED. BRIEF.md rule 5 names the
# transforms that push a shape's own solid voxels outside its own test — translate, rotate, around,
# repeat — and a displacement is the same failure by the same mechanism and a larger one: the
# surface is decided by coverage and the paint by a test at the cell's centre, and 0.030 of noise
# separates the two by up to a whole voxel. Missed, they fall through to the manifest's limestone
# base coat and the plaster comes back speckled with pale grit. 0.035 costs nothing here: the rule
# is keyed on the wall's own shape and there is no other part within 0.035 of it.
paint plaster where=vestibule_walls   below=0.035
paint plaster where=vestibule_ceiling below=0.02

# The inside of a coffer, grown enough to catch the voxels that line it rather than the air in it.
let vestibule_cof_face = round { vestibule_coffers } by=0.05
paint stucco where=vestibule_cof_face below=0.02

# --- the floor -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# All the zones are thin slabs from 1.70 to 1.83, straddling the floor plane so they catch the
# surface and nothing below it.
#
# The pattern is a field of nine squares of 0.75 (5M/3) — the largest square that divides the room
# BOTH ways, since 6.75 is 9 of them and 0.75 is the depth left after a border — alternating white
# marble and porphyry, each porphyry one carrying a marble lozenge. Round it a border 0.225 (M/2),
# which widens to 0.75 at the north where the screen's plinths stand on it.
#
# THE JOINTS ARE PAINTED AND NOT CUT: 0.03 m wide — M/15, the same number as the fillet between two
# flutes, ONE VOXEL at metre 32 — with no geometry underneath to tell a denoiser where the line
# goes. Half of this pattern is in the sun through the door and half is in shadow, in the same
# square metre, and that is the whole argument for putting it here rather than on a terrace.
#
# THIS IS THE ONE FLOOR IN THE BUILDING WHOSE JOINTS MUST STAY PAINT. rotunda.clip now cuts its own
# pavement into real stones with real 0.030 grooves and says why; here the ABSENCE of geometry under
# the line is the load, and the two rooms are four metres apart, so the comparison a denoiser has to
# pass is one turn of the head away rather than one screenshot away.
#
# THE FIELD DID NOT MOVE WHEN THE ROOM GREW, AND THAT IS WHERE THE 0.45 WAS TAKEN FROM. The room
# went from -6.60 .. -4.95 to -7.05 .. -4.95, so its middle went from -5.775 to -6.00 — and the field
# was already at -6.375 .. -5.625, centred on -6.00. The nine squares are now centred in the room
# instead of shoved against its north side, and the border came out 0.675 (1.5 M) at both ends
# instead of 0.225 south and 0.75 north. 0.675 is the screen plinth's own depth exactly, which is
# what the old 0.75 was reaching for and missing by a twentieth.
let vestibule_fl_zone = box -3.60 1.70 -7.05   3.60 1.83 -4.95
paint marble where=vestibule_fl_zone below=0.02

let vestibule_fl_field  = box -3.375 1.60 -6.375   3.375 1.93 -5.625
let vestibule_fl_border = difference { vestibule_fl_zone vestibule_fl_field }
paint porphyry where=vestibule_fl_border below=0.02

let vestibule_fl_sq   = box -0.375 1.70 -6.375   0.375 1.83 -5.625
let vestibule_fl_dark = mirror { translate { repeat { vestibule_fl_sq } x=1.50 nx=1 } 0.75 0 0 } axis=x
paint porphyry where=vestibule_fl_dark below=0.02

let vestibule_fl_diam = translate { rotate { box -0.21 1.70 -0.21   0.21 1.83 0.21 } y=0.125 } 0 0 -6.00
let vestibule_fl_loz  = mirror { translate { repeat { vestibule_fl_diam } x=1.50 nx=1 } 0.75 0 0 } axis=x
paint marble where=vestibule_fl_loz below=0.02

let vestibule_fl_bar   = box -0.015 1.70 -6.375   0.015 1.83 -5.625
let vestibule_fl_bars  = mirror { translate { repeat { vestibule_fl_bar } x=0.75 nx=4 } 0.375 0 0 } axis=x
let vestibule_fl_rail_s= box -3.375 1.70 -6.390   3.375 1.83 -6.360
let vestibule_fl_rail_n= box -3.375 1.70 -5.640   3.375 1.83 -5.610
let vestibule_fl_lines = union { vestibule_fl_bars vestibule_fl_rail_s vestibule_fl_rail_n }
paint verde where=vestibule_fl_lines below=0.02

# The threshold, in something hard and dark, because it is the one piece of floor in this room the
# sun lands on square and the one a foot lands on every time.
let vestibule_fl_sill = box -1.35 1.70 -7.55   1.35 1.83 -7.00
paint granite where=vestibule_fl_sill below=0.02

# AND THE TWO WORN PATCHES, IN `onyx`, WHICH IS WHAT WEAR ACTUALLY LOOKS LIKE.
#
# Not a darker colour — a SMOOTHER one. A million feet polish a stone long before they stain it, so
# the hollow at the door and the strip across to the screen are the same warm stone family as the
# floor around them at roughness 90 against granite's 225 and marble's 140. Onyx is also translucent
# at 160, which puts a subsurface material in the one square metre of this room the sun lands on
# square — the widest-dynamic-range argument at the top of this file, asked of a material rather
# than of a shape.
#
# THE ZONE IS THE HOLLOW GROWN INTO THE STONE AROUND IT AND NOT THE HOLLOW. Every voxel that lines
# a subtracted surface is a voxel the cutter MISSED — it is the first one outside — so a rule keyed
# on the cut itself paints the half of the lining that happens to fall inside and leaves the rest
# grey. `round { } by=0.06` reaches into the stone under the dish, where nothing can see it, and the
# clip to the floor slab keeps it off the wall. `below=0.035`: two ellipsoids placed by translation.
let vestibule_fl_worn = intersection {
    round { vestibule_wear } by=0.06
    box -1.35 1.70 -7.55   1.35 1.83 -5.40
}
paint onyx where=vestibule_fl_worn below=0.035

# --- the stone that stands on it -----------------------------------------------------------------
paint verde  where=vestibule_dado         below=0.02
paint marble where=vestibule_columns      below=0.02
paint marble where=vestibule_antae        below=0.02
paint marble where=vestibule_entab        below=0.02
paint marble where=vestibule_architrave   below=0.02

# The niche linings, grown the same way the coffers are.
let vestibule_niche_face = round { vestibule_niches } by=0.05
paint marble where=vestibule_niche_face below=0.02

# And the architrave round each of them, last of the stone, so it wins back the 0.045 of wall face
# the lining zone above reaches out onto. `below=0.035` — a `mirror` put it where it is.
paint marble where=vestibule_niche_dress below=0.035

# --- and the gilt, last --------------------------------------------------------------------------
paint gilt where=vestibule_rosettes below=0.02
